# Appendix: where the mixing comes from

In a horizontally homogeneous 1D column, horizontal tracer gradients and
vertical **fluid** velocity vanish, although wind-driven horizontal currents
can evolve. Turbulent diffusion mixes dissolved tracers vertically, while
particles also sink under gravity. See [Oceananigans boundary conditions](https://clima.github.io/OceananigansDocumentation/stable/models/boundary_conditions).

## 1. Classroom light and mixing

These curves are calculated from the **same functions** used in the workshop's
BATS and NABE biological simulations. They show how light and prescribed
seasonal mixing vary between sites.

WORKSHOP_STATION_FORCING_FIGURE

## 2. Vertical tracer diffusivity

The idealised diffusivity is strong in the mixed layer and weak below it. The
panels show the upper 300 m; the dashed curves mark the prescribed mixing
depth. Both figures are regenerated on every documentation build.

WORKSHOP_STATION_DIFFUSIVITY_FIGURE

## 3. Origin of the physical idealisation

The seasonal forcing was informed by 1D ocean simulations built with
[NumericalEarth.jl](https://numericalearth.github.io/NumericalEarthDocumentation/stable/)
and [Oceananigans' CATKE turbulence model](https://clima.github.io/OceananigansDocumentation/stable/models/turbulence_closures),
using [JRA55 atmospheric forcing](https://jra.kishou.go.jp/JRA-55/index_en.html)
and [EN4 initial temperature and salinity](https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/hadobs/en4/).
The source calculations are described in [`physics/README.md`](https://github.com/nanophyto/masts-asm-2026-modelling/blob/main/physics/README.md).
The classroom figures above are generated directly from the present-day
idealised forcing, so the published results remain reproducible with the
small inputs in this repository.
